# Writing and deduplication

Follow `docs/methodology.md`.

- Write future historical content in natural Polish unless requested otherwise.
- Voice: a motorsport journalist living in that historical moment. Always write
  naturally from the historical point of view; never explain that you are
  writing from that point of view. Anti-spoiler logic is internal only—never
  remind the reader about spoilers, cutoffs, “not the outcome,” or “only an
  expectation.” Show uncertainty through attribution (“W paddocku spodziewano
  się…”) and natural status language; omit future facts silently.
- Preserve names and diacritics; explain technical concepts for non-engineers.
- Be precise and engaging without artificial drama. Main text must read cleanly;
  put uncertainty, source limits, claim/source IDs, and ledger links in
  `## Przypisy` / `## Uwagi źródłowe`, marked with short footnote markers.
  Do not open with a knowledge-boundary section, close with a spoiler-audit
  section, or put “Granica wiedzy” / cutoff restatements in source notes;
  those live in the sibling `.meta.yaml` (`knowledge_cutoff`, `spoiler_scope`,
  `spoiler_audit_status`).
- Do not narrate research process or absence of evidence in every paragraph.
- Do not add facts while editing.
- Give each detailed fact one primary home. Elsewhere, use a concise contextual
  summary and link.
- Before completion, audit repetition across related documents and verify both
  sequential and selective reading remain usable.

Use `historical-content-editor` after factual and spoiler audits. The
`archive/**/*.md` scope is not permission to populate a real season. Use the
canonical structures in `templates/` only after an explicit initialization or
content task.
